      "text": "Let $r$ be an integer root of the polynomial $x^3 + 3x^2 + ax + 7 = 0.$  By the Rational Root Theorem, the only possible values of $r$ are 1, -1, 2, and -2.  In each case, we have\n\\begin{align*}\n(1)^3 &+ 3(1)^2 + a(1) + 7 = 0, \\\\\n(-1)^3 + 3(-1)^2 + a(-1) + 7 = 0, \\\\\n(2)^3 + 3(2)^2 + a(2) + 7 = 0, \\\\\n(-2)^3 + 3(-2)^2 + a(-2) + 7 = 0.\n\\end{align*}Simplifying each equation, we get\n\\begin{align*}\n1 + 3 + a + 7 &= 0, \\\\\n1 - 3 - a + 7 &= 0, \\\\\n8 + 12 + 2a + 7 &= 0, \\\\\n-8 + 12 - 2a + 7 &= 0.\n\\end{align*}Simplifying each equation further, we get\n\\begin{align*}\na + 11 &= -8, \\\\\n-2 - a &= -8, \\\\\na + 27 &= -8, \\\\\n-2 - a &= -8.\n\\end{align*}Solving each equation, we get $a = -30$, $a = 6$, $a = -35$, and $a = -6$.  Therefore, the possible integer values of $a$ are $\\boxed{-30, -6, -35, 6}$.The answer is: -30,-6,-35,6",
