      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "Given the polynomial \\( P(x) = x^3 + 3x^2 + ax + 7 \\) with at least one integer root, we need to find all integer values of \\( a \\) such that \\( P(x) = 0 \\) has an integer root. Let \\( r \\) be an integer root of the polynomial. Then \\( r \\) must satisfy:\n\n\\[ r^3 + 3r^2 + ar + 7 = 0. \\]\n\nRearranging this equation, we get:\n\n\\[ ar = -(r^3 + 3r^2 + 7). \\]\n\nThis implies:\n\\[ a = -\\frac{r^3 + 3r^2 + 7}{r}. \\]\n\nTo find suitable integer values for \\( a \\), we need \\( \\frac{r^3 + 3r^2 + 7}{r} \\) to be an integer for some integers \\( r \\). Let’s examine when \\( \\frac{r^3 + 3r^2 + 7}{r} \\) is an integer for various values of \\( r \\):\n\n1. \\( r = 0 \\):\n\n\\[\na = -\\frac{0^3 + 3 \\cdot 0^2 + 7}{0} \\quad (\\text{undefined, not valid as } 0 \\text{ can't divide 0})\n\\]\n\n2. \\( r = 1 \\):\n\n\\[\na = -\\frac{1^3 + 3 \\cdot 1^2 + 7}{1} = -\\frac{1 + 3 + 7}{1} = -\\frac{11}{1} = -11\n\\]\n\n3. \\( r = -1 \\):\n\n\\[\na = -\\frac{(-1)^3 + 3(-1)^2 + 7}{-1} = -\\frac{-1 + 3 + 7}{-1} = -\\frac{9}{-1} = 9\n\\]\n\n4. \\( r = 2 \\):\n\n\\[\na = -\\frac{2^3 + 3 \\cdot 2^2 + 7}{2} = -\\frac{8 + 12 + 7}{2} = -\\frac{27}{2} \\quad (\\text{not an integer})\n\\]\n\n5. \\( r = -2 \\):\n\n\\[\na = -\\frac{(-2)^3 + 3(-2)^2 + 7}{-2} = -\\frac{-8 + 12 + 7}{-2} = -\\frac{11}{-2} = \\frac{11}{2} \\quad (\\text{not an integer})\n\\]\n\n6. \\( r = 1/2 \\), \\( r = -1/2 \\), etc.: These values are not integers.\n\nFrom our analysis, the integer values of \\( a \\) can only be 9 and -11 from the above values of \\( r \\).\n\nHence, the integer values of \\( a \\) for which the polynomial \\( x^3 + 3x^2 + ax + 7 = 0 \\) has at least one integer root are:\n\n\\boxed{-11, 9}",
